```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

public class BinaryDataLoader<T> where T : new()
{
    private readonly string _filePath;
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, object> _cache = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, object>();
    private readonly BinaryFormatter _formatter = new BinaryFormatter();

    public BinaryDataLoader(string filePath)
    {
        _filePath = filePath;
    }

    public T LoadData(string key)
    {
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out var cachedObject))
        {
            return (T)cachedObject;
        }

        try
        {
            lock (_formatter)
            {
                using (var fileStream = new FileStream(_filePath, FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read, FileShare.ReadWrite))
                {
                    var data = _formatter.Deserialize(fileStream);
                    if (data is T typedData)
                    {
                        _cache.TryAdd(key, typedData);
                        return typedData;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Log the exception details
            Console.WriteLine($"Error loading data: {ex.Message}");
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to load data from binary file.", ex);
        }

        throw new InvalidOperationException("Invalid data type in binary file.");
    }
}
```

This C# function `BinaryDataLoader<T>` is a generic class designed to efficiently load and deserialize data from a binary file into a custom data structure of type T. It integrates with an existing logging system through the use of `Console.WriteLine` for error messages, which can be replaced with actual logging framework calls in a production environment.

The function uses a `ConcurrentDictionary<string, object>` as a caching mechanism to store frequently accessed data structures, reducing the need to load and deserialize them from the binary file repeatedly. This improves performance by minimizing file I/O operations.

The function is designed for concurrent access by multiple threads through the use of a lock on the `_formatter` object during deserialization. This ensures that only one thread can deserialize the binary file at a time, preventing race conditions and data corruption.

The function handles potential exceptions by catching any exceptions thrown during the file I/O operations or deserialization process. It logs the exception details using `Console.WriteLine`, which can be replaced with actual logging framework calls in a production environment. The function then throws an `InvalidOperationException` with a clear error message indicating that the data loading failed.

Overall, this C# function provides a thread-safe and efficient way to load and deserialize data from a binary file into a custom data structure, while also handling potential exceptions and providing clear error messages for debugging purposes.

